\date{2026-04-24T13:54:52Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Remark}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{We can visualize the situation by drawing a rectangular grid, where each row corresponds to one option for the first choice, and each column corresponds to an option for the second choice. Then each grid square corresponds to a unique combination of choices. The fact that the choices are \em{independent} corresponds to the fact that the picture is perfectly rectangular.}

\diagram{200}{*}{\verb<<<|
attach v d d2 = cat' v (with & sep .~ norm v) [ d2, d ]
dia = vcat (replicate 5 (hcat (replicate 4 (square 1))))
  # centerXY
  # attach unit_X (text "c1")
  # attach unitY (text "c2")
  # fontSizeL 0.5
  # frame 1
<<<}

\p{This really boils down to the fact, which we [saw before when learning about sets](0079), that ##{|A \times B| = |A| \times |B|.}}
